/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · philosophy batch y */
WB.L("trolley-problem", "philosophy", "beginner", "The trolley problem: would you pull the lever?",
  "A runaway tram, five people on the track and one lever in your hand. This simple puzzle has become one of the most argued-about scenarios in moral philosophy, and it now shapes how we think about self-driving cars.",
  [
    ["The puzzle", "The trolley problem asks whether it is right to divert a runaway tram so it kills one person instead of five.",
      "Picture yourself beside a railway line. A tram with failed brakes is hurtling towards five workers who cannot get out of the way. You stand next to a lever. Pull it, and the tram swings onto a side track, where it will kill one worker instead. Do nothing, and five people die.\n\nMost people say they would pull the lever. But the puzzle is not really about trams. It is a carefully built thought experiment, a stripped-down scenario that isolates one question so we can examine our moral instincts. Do the numbers alone matter, or does how a death comes about matter too?\n\nThe British philosopher Philippa Foot introduced it in 1967, and Judith Jarvis Thomson later named it and invented many variations."],
    ["The footbridge twist", "When saving five means pushing one person to their death, most people's answer flips even though the arithmetic is identical.",
      "Thomson's most famous variation puts you on a footbridge above the track. There is no lever. The only way to stop the tram is to push a very large stranger off the bridge into its path. His body would halt it, saving five, but he would die.\n\nThe arithmetic is identical: one life for five. Yet in surveys, most people who would pull the lever say they would not push the man. Many find pushing deeply wrong, even if they struggle to explain why. That gap makes the puzzle so productive. If the numbers are the same, something else must be driving our judgement, and philosophers have spent decades working out what it is, and whether it should count."],
    ["The case for counting", "Consequentialists judge actions by their outcomes, so they favour saving five in both versions, though critics say this could justify terrible acts.",
      "One family of answers is consequentialism: the rightness of an action depends on its results. The best-known version, utilitarianism, associated with Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill, aims for the greatest overall wellbeing. In the lever case, five deaths are worse than one, so you should pull.\n\nA consistent consequentialist may say the same about the footbridge, arguing our reluctance to push is a gut reaction, not a moral insight. Defenders note the approach is impartial and takes every life equally seriously. Critics reply that if outcomes are all that matter, it could justify terrible things, such as harvesting one healthy patient's organs to save five others, which almost everyone rejects."],
    ["The case for limits", "Some thinkers argue people must never be used merely as a means, and the doctrine of double effect separates intended harm from foreseen side effects.",
      "A rival tradition, linked to Immanuel Kant, holds that people must never be treated merely as a means to an end. Pushing the man uses his body as a tool to stop the tram, which looks like exactly that. Pulling the lever is different: the one worker dies as a side effect of redirecting the threat, not as the means of stopping it.\n\nThis is the heart of the doctrine of double effect, which traces back to the medieval thinker Thomas Aquinas. It says causing harm as a foreseen side effect can sometimes be permissible when causing the same harm intentionally would not. Critics question whether that distinction can carry so much moral weight."],
    ["Brains and machines", "Emotion and reasoning both shape moral choices, and self-driving cars have turned the thought experiment into a real design question.",
      "Around 2001, Joshua Greene and colleagues scanned people's brains as they considered trolley dilemmas. Up-close, personal harms like pushing seemed to trigger stronger emotional responses, while impersonal cases like the lever invited calculation. Greene proposed a 'dual process' account in which both systems compete. Some later studies have questioned how well the results hold up, and philosophers ask whether the emotion tracks something real.\n\nThen came autonomous vehicles. Someone must decide in advance what software does when a crash is unavoidable. The 2018 Moral Machine project from MIT collected millions of responses worldwide. It found broad agreement on sparing more lives and humans over animals, but regional differences on other questions. Engineers stress that real crashes are rarely neat dilemmas, and avoiding collisions is the goal."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the trolley problem designed to test?", ["Whether numbers alone decide right, or the manner of a death counts too","How well people understand tram safety and track signals","Which railway design is cheapest to build and maintain","Whether people can follow instructions under time pressure"], 0, "The scenario isolates one question so we can examine our moral instincts."],
    [1, "In the footbridge version, how do most people respond compared with the lever version?", ["They are more willing to act","They are less willing to act","They respond exactly the same","They refuse to answer"], 1, "Though the numbers are identical, most people who would pull the lever would not push the man."],
    [2, "What does a consequentialist mainly use to judge whether an action is right?", ["The intentions of the actor","Religious rules","Its outcomes","Tradition"], 2, "Consequentialists weigh results, so saving five usually beats saving one."],
    [3, "What does the doctrine of double effect say?", ["Every action has two equal effects, one good and one bad","Two wrongs together can sometimes make a right","Only the second effect of an action counts morally","Foreseen harm can be allowed where intended harm would not"], 3, "It separates harm used as a means from harm that is a foreseen side effect."],
    [4, "What did the Moral Machine project find?", ["Broad agreement on sparing more lives, but regional differences","Near-total agreement worldwide on every question it asked","Most people wanted cars to ignore pedestrians entirely","Engineers rejected the idea of the experiment completely"], 0, "It showed shared intuitions about numbers but disagreement on other trade-offs."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Five lives or one, and why pushing feels different from pulling a lever."});

WB.L("ship-of-theseus", "philosophy", "beginner", "The ship of Theseus: what makes you the same person?",
  "Replace every plank of a ship, one by one, and is it still the same ship? This ancient puzzle leads straight to one of the most personal questions in philosophy: what makes you, you, over a lifetime of change?",
  [
    ["A ship of new planks", "If every part of an object is gradually replaced, it is unclear whether it is still the same object.",
      "The Greek writer Plutarch, around the turn of the second century, told of the ship in which the hero Theseus was said to have sailed home from Crete. The Athenians kept it for generations, removing old timbers as they decayed and fitting new ones. Philosophers used it in arguments about growth: some said it stayed the same ship, others that it did not.\n\nCarry the process to the end, with not one original plank left. Is it still Theseus's ship? If yes, what makes it so? If no, at which plank did it stop being the original? Neither answer is as comfortable as it first seems."],
    ["Hobbes's twist", "Rebuilding the ship from the old planks shows that 'the same thing' bundles features, such as history and material, that can come apart.",
      "In the 1600s the English philosopher Thomas Hobbes added a complication. Suppose someone collected every old plank as it was removed and later reassembled them, in order, into a ship. Now there are two. One sits in the harbour, continuously maintained, with no original material. The other is built entirely of the original timbers.\n\nWhich is the ship of Theseus? Both have a strong claim: one has continuity of history and use, the other continuity of material. Our everyday idea of 'the same thing' bundles features that usually travel together but can come apart."],
    ["You are a ship too", "Because your body and mind keep changing, the puzzle becomes the problem of personal identity, which asks what makes you the same person over time.",
      "You are a bit like that ship. Many of your cells are replaced over time, from skin to blood to the lining of your gut, although some, including most neurons in the brain, can last a lifetime. Your beliefs, tastes and memories change even more. The child in your earliest photos shares little with you in material, knowledge or personality, yet you feel certain that child was you.\n\nPhilosophers call this the problem of personal identity. It matters for everyday things, like holding someone responsible for a promise or a crime, and for deeper ones, like what it would mean to survive."],
    ["Memory and its limits", "Locke tied identity to memory, but Reid's brave officer showed that memory alone breaks the logic of 'same as'.",
      "In the late 1600s John Locke argued that a person is a thinking being who can consider itself as itself at different times. You are the same person as someone in the past if you remember that person's experiences from the inside, not because you share a body. That explains why memory loss feels so frightening.\n\nNearly a century later, Thomas Reid offered the brave officer. A boy is flogged for stealing; as an officer he remembers the flogging; as a general he remembers being the officer but has forgotten the flogging. On Locke's view the general is the officer and the officer is the boy, yet the general is not the boy. Later philosophers repaired the theory with overlapping chains of memory and psychological connection."],
    ["Is there a fixed self?", "Buddhist non-self and Parfit's teleporter both suggest that what matters is continuity of mind, not an unchanging core self.",
      "Long before Locke, Buddhist thinkers took another route. In the Questions of King Milinda, the monk Nagasena asks the king whether his chariot is the axle, the wheels, the frame or the reins. None alone is the chariot, nor is it separate from them: 'chariot' names parts working together. Likewise 'Nagasena' names a changing combination of body, feelings, perceptions and mental activity, with no unchanging core. This idea, anatta or non-self, is central to many Buddhist traditions.\n\nIn 1984 Derek Parfit imagined a teleporter that destroys you and builds an exact copy on Mars. Is that you? He concluded there may be no fact of the matter, and that psychological continuity matters more than identity. Many philosophers disagree."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What difficulty does the ship of Theseus puzzle raise?", ["Whether old wooden ships were stronger than modern ones","Whether an object stays the same after all its parts are replaced","Whether a ship can be repaired without losing its value","Whether the planks of an old ship can ever be counted"], 1, "The puzzle asks what makes an object the same thing when none of its original material remains."],
    [1, "What does Hobbes's rebuilt ship reveal about the idea of 'the same thing'?", ["It bundles features like history and material that can split","It depends only on how old the original material is","It always follows what the original builder intended","It is settled by whichever of the two ships is larger"], 0, "Two ships each match part of 'sameness', so the idea turns out to bundle several features."],
    [2, "Why does the ship puzzle apply to people?", ["Our bodies stay fixed while only our thoughts change","We change in body and mind, yet feel we are the same person","Every part of the brain is replaced every single year","People are legally treated as new people each decade"], 1, "Because we change in body and mind, we need an account of what keeps a person the same over a lifetime."],
    [3, "What was Reid's objection to Locke's memory view?", ["Memory is always accurate, so identity never changes in any way","It makes the general the officer, the officer the boy, but not the boy","Anyone who forgets nothing is no longer counted as a person","Memory belongs to the body and cannot be a feature of the mind"], 1, "Memory links can fail to chain together, which breaks the basic logic of 'same as'."],
    [4, "What is the Buddhist idea of non-self, as in the chariot example?", ["A person is a soul that moves between bodies unchanged","A person is defined only by their role in society","A person is simply whatever their memory records","A person is a changing bundle of parts with no fixed core"], 3, "Like 'chariot', 'self' is a name for parts working together, not a separate fixed thing."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Replace every plank of a ship, and is it still the same ship? Are you?"});

WB.L("descartes-doubt", "philosophy", "beginner", "Descartes: I think, therefore I am",
  "What if everything you believe is a dream, or a trick? René Descartes set out to doubt everything he possibly could, and what was left became one of the most famous sentences ever written.",
  [
    ["Doubting everything", "Descartes set out to clear away every doubtful belief and rebuild knowledge on a foundation that could not be doubted.",
      "In the winter of 1619, a young Frenchman named René Descartes was stuck in Germany, serving with an army. By his own account, he spent a day alone in a stove-heated room, thinking. Despite an excellent education, he felt much of what he had learned was uncertain. Admiring the step-by-step certainty of geometry, he resolved to clear away every belief that could be doubted and see what solid ground remained.\n\nIn his Meditations of 1641 he began with the senses. A tower looks round from far away and square up close; a straight stick looks bent in water. A source that has fooled you once is not safe to trust fully. This was a tool for one quiet hour of reflection, not a way to live as a sceptic."],
    ["The dream and the demon", "Descartes asked how we could know we are not dreaming, or being fooled by a powerful deceiver.",
      "Descartes then raised a stronger worry. He had often dreamed he was sitting by the fire in his dressing gown when he was actually asleep in bed. Is there any sure sign that separates waking life from a vivid dream? If not, even the scene in front of him might be unreal.\n\nFinally he imagined an evil demon, supremely powerful and cunning, devoting all its energy to deceiving him about everything, perhaps even simple sums. Modern philosophers have updated the demon into a brain in a vat, fed false experiences by scientists, an idea that inspired films such as The Matrix. The question is the same: can you prove this is not happening to you right now?"],
    ["The one certainty", "Even if he is deceived about everything else, Descartes must exist in order to be deceived: I think, therefore I am.",
      "Here Descartes found his foothold. Suppose the demon deceives him about everything. Still, to be deceived, he must exist. To doubt, he must be thinking. However far the doubt is pushed, it cannot reach the fact that there is a doubter.\n\nIn the Meditations he put it as 'I am, I exist', which must be true whenever he thinks it. In his earlier Discourse on the Method of 1637, written in French, he used the form that became famous, usually rendered in Latin as cogito, ergo sum: I think, therefore I am. The early Christian thinker Augustine had made a similar point more than a thousand years earlier, arguing that if he was mistaken, he must exist to be mistaken."],
    ["Rebuilding, and mind and body", "Descartes tried to rebuild trust in the world through God and argued that mind and body are distinct, but both steps drew sharp criticism.",
      "Knowing only that you exist is a lonely position. Descartes argued that his idea of a perfect being could only come from a perfect God, who would not deceive him, so clear and distinct perceptions could be trusted. Critics noted the Cartesian circle: he seems to use clear ideas to prove God, then God to guarantee clear ideas.\n\nHis method also led to dualism. He could doubt having a body but not that he was thinking, so mind must be a different kind of thing from body. From 1643 Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed him: if the mind is not physical, how can it move an arm? He suggested the pineal gland in the brain, an answer few found satisfying. Most philosophers and scientists today reject his dualism."],
    ["Why he still matters", "Descartes made the question of what we can know for certain central to modern philosophy, and his method of careful doubt still helps.",
      "Descartes died in Stockholm in 1650, not long after moving there at the invitation of Queen Christina of Sweden. By starting from the individual mind and asking what it can know for certain, he put epistemology, the study of knowledge, at the centre of philosophy for centuries. Later thinkers such as John Locke and David Hume answered him by stressing experience over pure reason.\n\nHe also helped shape modern science and gave us coordinate geometry, the familiar x and y axes, called Cartesian coordinates. Even people who reject his conclusions use his method: when in doubt, slow down, question your assumptions and ask what you really have good reason to believe."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Descartes trying to do with his method of doubt?", ["Persuade people to stop trusting their senses in daily life","Prove that geometry was less reliable than experience","Clear away doubtful beliefs to find a certain foundation","Show that all earlier scholars were equally wrong"], 2, "The doubt was a tool to find out what could survive the strongest possible doubt."],
    [1, "What point does the evil demon thought experiment make?", ["Evil demons are the best explanation for vivid dreams","Waking life is always clearly different from dreaming","Experience alone cannot prove we are not being deceived","Simple sums are the one thing we can never doubt"], 2, "If a deceiver could fake everything, no sensation alone proves the world is as it seems."],
    [2, "Why can't even the demon deceive Descartes about his own existence?", ["To be deceived, he must exist and be thinking","The demon is not powerful enough to fool him","His senses confirm to him that he has a body","Mathematics alone proves that he must exist"], 0, "Doubting or being deceived requires a thinker, so 'I think, therefore I am' survives the doubt."],
    [3, "What is the Cartesian circle objection?", ["He proves God exists by relying only on the senses","He proves God from clear ideas, then clear ideas from God","He first doubts his own existence and then proves it","He relies on his dreams to prove the world is real"], 1, "The argument seems to assume what it needs to prove."],
    [4, "How does Descartes's method still help us today?", ["By slowing down and asking what we have good reason to believe","By teaching us to distrust every source of information","By proving that reason is more reliable than experience","By showing that certainty is always easy to reach"], 0, "His lasting contribution is a habit of careful, reasoned doubt."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A man in a warm room tried to doubt everything. One sentence survived."});

WB.L("epicurus-pleasure", "philosophy", "beginner", "Epicurus: the surprising philosophy of pleasure",
  "Today 'epicurean' suggests fine wine and rich food. But Epicurus, the philosopher behind the word, lived simply in a garden, ate bread and water, and taught that the secret of happiness was needing less.",
  [
    ["The Garden", "Around 306 BC Epicurus founded a simple school in a garden near Athens that valued friendship and welcomed women and enslaved people.",
      "Today 'epicurean' suggests fine dining, but Epicurus lived simply. Born on the Greek island of Samos in 341 BC, he later bought a house with a garden near Athens, and his followers became known as the Garden. Ancient sources say it welcomed women and enslaved people, who were usually excluded from philosophical schools. Members lived simply and valued friendship above all; Epicurus is reported to have called it the greatest thing wisdom provides.\n\nThe ancient biographer Diogenes Laertius says Epicurus lived mainly on bread and water, and once asked a friend to send a little pot of cheese so he could have a feast whenever he liked."],
    ["Pleasure means calm", "Epicurus taught that pleasure is the goal of life, but he meant mainly freedom from pain and worry.",
      "Epicurus held that pleasure is the beginning and end of a happy life. His enemies, ancient and modern, presented that as an invitation to indulge. But he defined pleasure in a particular way. The highest pleasure was a calm state of body and mind: freedom from physical pain, called aponia, and freedom from mental disturbance, called ataraxia.\n\nA banquet might bring a burst of enjoyment, followed by a hangover, debt or jealousy. A simple meal with friends, eaten without anxiety, could bring deeper and more lasting contentment. In his Letter to Menoeceus, he says explicitly that by pleasure he does not mean the pleasures of the profligate."],
    ["Sorting your desires", "Epicurus sorted desires into natural and necessary, natural but unnecessary, and empty ones that can never be satisfied.",
      "A practical tool at the heart of Epicureanism is a way of sorting desires. Some are natural and necessary, like food, shelter and friendship. These are easy to satisfy and bring real peace. Some are natural but not necessary, like fancy food: fine to enjoy when they come along, but not worth chasing.\n\nA third kind he called empty or vain, like the desire for fame, power or limitless wealth. These are dangerous because they have no natural limit. However much you get, there is always more to want, so they breed endless anxiety. The lesson sounds strikingly modern. Advertising and social media often work by turning unnecessary desires into what feel like needs."],
    ["Nothing to fear", "Epicurus argued that fear of the gods and of death is needless, because nature runs on atoms and death ends all sensation.",
      "Epicurus built his ethics on the atomism of Democritus: everything is made of indivisible atoms moving through empty space. Thunderstorms, earthquakes and disease have natural causes. He did not deny that gods existed, but argued that perfectly happy beings would not bother to punish or reward humans.\n\nDeath was next. In the Letter to Menoeceus he says death is nothing to us, since good and bad lie in sensation and death ends sensation. When we exist, death is not present; when death is present, we do not exist. The Roman poet Lucretius, in On the Nature of Things, added that we do not fear the endless time before we were born, so why fear the time after? Critics reply that what we dread is losing the good things life would have held."],
    ["The fourfold remedy", "Epicureans condensed their teaching into a four-part remedy for anxiety and advised a quiet life, which drew both admiration and criticism.",
      "Later followers condensed the teaching into the tetrapharmakos, or fourfold remedy, found in the writings of Philodemus. Commonly translated: don't fear god, don't worry about death, what is good is easy to get, and what is terrible is easy to endure. The last line reflects Epicurus's view that intense pain tends to be brief while lasting pain is usually bearable, which may seem too optimistic for chronic illness. Treating philosophy as medicine for the mind echoes modern therapy.\n\nEpicurus also advised 'living unnoticed', away from politics, which he saw as breeding fear and rivalry. The Stoics and Cicero called this selfish. Yet Thomas Jefferson called himself an Epicurean late in life. The question stays fresh: what do you actually need to be content?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Epicurus actually live?", ["Lavishly, hosting grand banquets for his followers","Alone, avoiding all friendships and all visitors","In public life, advising the leaders of Athens","Simply, on bread and water, among friends in a garden"], 3, "The man whose name suggests luxury treated a little cheese as a feast."],
    [1, "What did Epicurus mean by pleasure?", ["The intense enjoyment of food, wine and entertainment","Winning honour and lasting respect from others","Taking in as many new experiences as possible","Mainly freedom from physical pain and mental disturbance"], 3, "The highest pleasure, for Epicurus, was calm of body and mind, not indulgence."],
    [2, "Why did Epicurus see empty desires, such as for fame or limitless wealth, as dangerous?", ["They always damage the health of the body","They can never be shared with close friends","They have no natural limit, so they breed anxiety","They are impossible for anyone to feel naturally"], 2, "Because there is always more to want, they can never be satisfied."],
    [3, "What was Epicurus's argument that death is nothing to us?", ["The soul is judged after death and then rewarded or punished","Death is painful but brief, and so it is bearable for most people","Good and bad lie in sensation, and death ends all sensation","Fear of death keeps people honest and so is useful to society"], 2, "When death is present, we do not exist, so death cannot harm us."],
    [4, "Why did the Stoics and Cicero criticise the Epicureans?", ["They said Epicureans ate far too lavishly","They thought they believed in far too many gods","They called them selfish for neglecting civic duty","They said they spent too much time in politics"], 2, "Epicurus's advice to avoid public life looked to critics like a neglect of civic duty."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The man behind 'epicurean' lived on bread, water and a little cheese."});

WB.L("ubuntu-philosophy", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Ubuntu: I am because we are",
  "A person is a person through other people. This idea from southern Africa helped guide a nation through its transition from apartheid, and it offers a challenge to the way many Western philosophies picture the self.",
  [
    ["I see you", "Ubuntu is a southern African idea that we become fully human through our relationships with others.",
      "In many parts of southern Africa, a common greeting translates roughly as 'I see you', and the reply as 'I am here'. The exchange hints at a philosophy in which existence is affirmed through recognition by others. It is often summed up in the word ubuntu, found in Nguni languages such as isiZulu and isiXhosa, with related words across many Bantu languages.\n\nUbuntu is usually translated as 'humanness' or 'humanity towards others', and captured in the isiZulu saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu: 'a person is a person through other persons'. It is not one written theory by a founding philosopher. It is a living ethical outlook, carried in proverbs, customs and everyday expectations, which thinkers have more recently set out systematically."],
    ["The relational self", "Ubuntu treats personhood as something achieved through relationships, not simply possessed by an isolated individual.",
      "Many influential Western philosophies start with the individual. Descartes began with a single mind doubting everything; social contract theorists imagined separate individuals agreeing to form a society. Ubuntu starts elsewhere, treating people as fundamentally bound up with one another. On many accounts, personhood is not just a biological fact but something you grow into by how you treat others.\n\nIn everyday speech in some communities, a person who behaves cruelly or selfishly may be said to lack ubuntu, or even to be 'not a person', meaning not living up to what a human should be. The Kenyan-born philosopher John Mbiti put a related African communal outlook in 1969: 'I am, because we are; and since we are, therefore I am.'"],
    ["What it asks of you", "Ubuntu values generosity, hospitality and compassion, and judges actions by whether they promote harmonious relationships.",
      "In practice, ubuntu is associated with sharing with those in need, welcoming strangers, respecting elders, and repairing damaged relationships rather than simply punishing. A frequently told illustration is a traveller passing through a village, who would expect to be offered food and water without having to ask.\n\nThe American-born philosopher Thaddeus Metz, who works in South Africa, has tried to state its core precisely. On an ubuntu view, he suggests, right actions respect and promote harmonious relationships, where people identify with one another and act for each other's good. Wrong actions are unfriendly or divisive. That makes ubuntu different from both rule-based and outcome-based ethics: the focus is the quality of relationships."],
    ["Reconciliation and law", "Ubuntu shaped South Africa's transition from apartheid, from the Truth and Reconciliation Commission to the abolition of the death penalty.",
      "Ubuntu became internationally known during South Africa's transition to democracy. The 1993 interim Constitution said there was 'a need for ubuntu but not for victimisation'. Archbishop Desmond Tutu, who chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, explained his approach through ubuntu. The commission offered amnesty to some perpetrators who fully disclosed politically motivated crimes, and gave victims a public platform. Tutu argued that dehumanising others diminishes the oppressor too, and that healing requires restoring relationships. Some victims felt justice was sacrificed.\n\nIn 1995, in S v Makwanyane, the Constitutional Court ruled the death penalty unconstitutional. Several judges cited ubuntu as valuing life and dignity and favouring reconciliation over retribution. Critics say the concept is too broad to guide judges clearly."],
    ["Debates and wider voices", "Critics call ubuntu vague or romanticised, and it is one strand in a wider tradition of African philosophy.",
      "Ubuntu has thoughtful critics, including African philosophers. Some say it has become a slogan, used in corporate training until it means little more than 'teamwork'. Others warn against romanticising a single 'African' worldview across hundreds of cultures. A deeper worry concerns rights: if personhood depends on community, what protects dissenters or people marginalised by their own communities? Defenders reply that ubuntu condemns treating anyone as less than human. Kwame Gyekye proposed a 'moderate communitarianism' giving both community and individual rights their due.\n\nOther African philosophers include Kwasi Wiredu, who urged 'conceptual decolonisation', and Henry Odera Oruka, who recorded the reasoning of local sages. Ubuntu leaves a question worth asking: how much of who you are was made by other people?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the isiZulu saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu mean?", ["A person is a person through other persons","A person is stronger when alone","A leader is a leader through the law","A person is defined by their ancestors"], 0, "Ubuntu holds that we become fully human through our relationships with others."],
    [1, "How does ubuntu differ from philosophies that start with the isolated individual?", ["It denies that individual people exist at all","It sees personhood as grown through relationships","It holds that personhood is purely biological","It says community matters only to the village elders"], 1, "For ubuntu, being a person is an achievement of relationship, not a private possession."],
    [2, "According to Thaddeus Metz, what makes an action right on an ubuntu view?", ["It produces the greatest total happiness","It respects and promotes harmonious relationships","It follows a fixed rule without exception","It benefits the person who acts on it"], 1, "Ubuntu focuses on the quality of relationships rather than rules or outcomes alone."],
    [3, "How did Tutu's Truth and Reconciliation Commission reflect ubuntu?", ["Reliance only on trials and harsh punishment of every offender","An effort to erase the past from the nation's public memory","Immediate legal immunity for every perpetrator without disclosure","Amnesty for full disclosure, plus a public voice for victims"], 3, "Tutu argued that healing requires restoring relationships, not only punishment."],
    [4, "What is one criticism of ubuntu raised by African philosophers?", ["It has no real connection to everyday life or ordinary expectations","It places too much emphasis on the individual rights of each person","It rejects every form of community and shared responsibility","It may be vague or romanticised, and may pressure dissenters to conform"], 3, "Critics worry the concept can become a slogan or fail to protect individuals."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Ubuntu says you are a person only through other people. What does that change?"});

WB.L("islamic-golden-age-philosophy", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Philosophy's golden age in the Islamic world",
  "Between Ancient Greece and the European Renaissance, some of the boldest philosophy on Earth was written in Arabic. Meet the thinkers who preserved Aristotle, argued about cause and effect, and imagined a man floating in the air.",
  [
    ["Translating the Greeks", "From the late 700s, scholars under the Abbasid caliphs translated Greek philosophy and science into Arabic, and argued with it.",
      "In the late 700s and 800s, under the Abbasid caliphs based in Baghdad, a remarkable translation movement got under way. Scholars, many of them Christians fluent in Greek and Syriac, translated Aristotle, Plato's ideas as passed down by later writers, and Greek medicine, astronomy and mathematics into Arabic. Wealthy patrons paid well. Baghdad's House of Wisdom is often associated with the movement, although historians debate how far it was a formal academy rather than a palace library.\n\nMuch of this learning was scarcely read in Western Europe at the time. It became part of a lively intellectual culture stretching from Central Asia to Spain. Thinkers did not just preserve these texts; they argued with them."],
    ["The first philosophers", "Al-Kindi and al-Farabi set out to show how Greek philosophy related to Islamic belief and to politics.",
      "Al-Kindi, working in Baghdad in the 800s, is often called 'the philosopher of the Arabs'. He argued that truth should be welcomed whatever its source, even foreign peoples, and that philosophy and revelation pointed in the same direction.\n\nA generation or two later, al-Farabi, who probably came from Central Asia, became so respected as an interpreter of Aristotle that he was called 'the Second Teacher'. He wrote on logic, music and especially politics. Inspired partly by Plato's Republic, he described a 'virtuous city' led by a wise ruler who would guide citizens towards true happiness. His work shaped how later thinkers connected philosophy, religion and the good society."],
    ["Avicenna's floating man", "Avicenna distinguished essence from existence and imagined a floating man to argue that we know our own existence directly.",
      "Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was born around 980 near Bukhara, in today's Uzbekistan. By his own account he mastered most sciences by eighteen. His Canon of Medicine served as a medical textbook in Europe for centuries. In philosophy, he distinguished what a thing is, its essence, from the fact that it exists: you can understand a unicorn without knowing whether any exist. He argued for a necessary being, whose essence includes existence, identified with God.\n\nHis best-known thought experiment imagines a person created in mid-air, blindfolded, limbs apart, with no sensations. Would they know they exist? Avicenna said yes, taking this to show the self is distinct from the body. It resembles Descartes's argument six centuries later. Critics question whether imagining yourself without a body shows you could exist without one."],
    ["Al-Ghazali and Averroes", "Al-Ghazali questioned whether we ever observe necessary cause and effect, and Averroes defended philosophy in reply.",
      "Not everyone welcomed the philosophers' confidence. In the late 1000s, Abu Hamid al-Ghazali wrote The Incoherence of the Philosophers, attacking Avicenna and others. His most striking argument concerned causation. When cotton touches fire, it burns. But we observe only that burning follows contact, never a necessary connection. He concluded that God directly creates each event, usually in regular patterns, so miracles are possible. Centuries later David Hume made a similar observation for very different reasons.\n\nIn the 1100s, in Córdoba, the judge and physician Ibn Rushd, or Averroes, replied with The Incoherence of the Incoherence. He argued that religious law itself encouraged capable people to study nature through reason. His commentaries on Aristotle became so influential in Latin translation that Thomas Aquinas called him simply 'the Commentator'."],
    ["A shared inheritance", "This tradition linked Greek, Jewish, Christian and Muslim thinkers and did not end in the 1100s.",
      "This was not only a Muslim story. The Jewish philosopher Moses Maimonides, born in Córdoba and later working in Egypt, wrote his Guide for the Perplexed in Arabic, engaging closely with Aristotle and Islamic philosophy. Christian scholars in Spain translated Arabic works into Latin, feeding the rise of medieval European universities.\n\nIbn Tufayl wrote a philosophical novel about a boy raised alone on an island who reasons his way to deep truths. It may have been known in Europe by the time of Robinson Crusoe.\n\nIt is also a myth that Islamic philosophy ended in the 1100s. It continued to flourish, especially in Persia, with thinkers such as Mulla Sadra in the 1600s. These debates on reason, faith and the self belong to everyone's intellectual history."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Abbasid-era translators help achieve?", ["Greek philosophy was replaced by entirely new Arabic ideas","Greek texts were kept hidden from most scholars and readers","Greek learning became part of a lively Arabic culture that debated the texts","Works were produced mainly in Latin for a European audience"], 2, "Scholars did not just preserve Greek works; they engaged with and debated them."],
    [1, "What did al-Kindi argue about truth?", ["Welcome truth from any source; philosophy and revelation agree","Truth should come only from scholars of one's own people","Truth cannot be reached through human reason or philosophy","Foreign ideas should be avoided because they mislead believers"], 0, "Al-Kindi held that truth is truth wherever it comes from."],
    [2, "What does Avicenna's floating man suggest?", ["The body is the whole of what a person really is","A person without sensations would still be self-aware","Thinking is impossible without constant sensory input","People can never be sure that they exist at all"], 1, "He took this to show that the self is something distinct from the body."],
    [3, "What did al-Ghazali claim about cause and effect?", ["We see burning follow contact but never a necessary connection forcing it","Natural laws are fixed and can never be questioned by anyone","Fire never causes burning, because cotton cannot burn at all","Causes are plainly visible to any careful observer of nature"], 0, "His point was that we see sequence, not necessity, which left room for miracles."],
    [4, "Why is it a myth that Islamic philosophy ended in the 1100s?", ["It was replaced by the Greek philosophy it had once translated","It continued to flourish, for example in Persia with Mulla Sadra in the 1600s","It moved entirely to Europe and disappeared from the Islamic world","It was banned across the region by the caliphs of the period"], 1, "Philosophical debate in the Islamic world carried on for centuries after Averroes."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some of history's boldest philosophy was written in Arabic. Meet the thinkers."});

WB.L("problem-of-induction", "philosophy", "advanced", "Hume's problem: why trust that the sun will rise?",
  "You are sure the sun will rise tomorrow. But can you prove it? David Hume's simple question about learning from experience has troubled philosophers and scientists for nearly three centuries, and a black swan from Western Australia became its mascot.",
  [
    ["The question nobody asked", "Hume asked what justifies our expectation that the future will resemble the past, a question about induction.",
      "In 1739 a young Scottish philosopher, David Hume, published A Treatise of Human Nature, which he later said 'fell dead-born from the press'. In it, and in a 1748 book, he asked a question that seems almost silly. Why do we believe the sun will rise tomorrow? Because it always has. But we have only observed the past. Our confidence rests on an assumption that nature will keep behaving as it has.\n\nThere are two kinds of reasoning. In deduction, true premises guarantee the conclusion. Induction moves from observed cases to rules or predictions, so the conclusion could still be false. Science, medicine and crossing the road depend on it. Hume did not say stop using it. He asked whether we can justify it without assuming what we are trying to prove."],
    ["The circle", "Any argument that induction works seems to rely on induction itself, which is circular.",
      "Hume's argument runs roughly like this. A justification for induction must be either a deductive proof or an argument from experience. It cannot be deductive, because there is no contradiction in imagining the future being different: you can coherently picture the sun failing to rise, or bread ceasing to nourish.\n\nSo it must come from experience. But that argument would be: induction has worked in the past, so it will work in the future. That is itself inductive, so it assumes the very thing in question. Hume concluded that our expectations come not from reason but from custom or habit. After seeing one event follow another many times, the mind simply comes to expect it. This, he thought, is how human nature works. It is now called the problem of induction, and it remains famously unsolved."],
    ["Chickens and black swans", "Russell's chicken and Australia's black swans show how expectations built on past experience can fail.",
      "Bertrand Russell gave a grim illustration in 1912. A chicken is fed every day by the farmer and comes to expect food whenever he appears. One day, the farmer wrings its neck instead. Its past experience was real, but it misled the chicken completely.\n\nA more cheerful example comes from Australia. For centuries Europeans used 'black swan' for something impossible, because every swan they had seen was white. In 1697 the Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh's expedition recorded black swans on a river in Western Australia, which they named the Swan River. A rule supported by countless observations was overturned by one. The black swan is now a common emblem of the limits of induction."],
    ["Popper and grue", "Popper said science advances by trying to falsify bold theories, while Goodman's 'grue' showed that choosing which patterns to trust is itself puzzling.",
      "The philosopher Karl Popper, in work first published in 1934, agreed that induction cannot be justified but argued science does not need it. Scientists do not prove theories by piling up confirming cases. They propose bold conjectures and try to refute them. One genuine black swan falsifies 'all swans are white', and that is deduction. Surviving theories are simply the best we have so far. Critics reply that we still rely on tested theories to predict the future, which brings induction back.\n\nIn 1955 Nelson Goodman defined 'grue' as green if first observed before some future date, and blue otherwise. Every emerald observed so far is both green and grue, so the evidence seems to support opposite predictions. We should project green, but why? The hard part is knowing which patterns count."],
    ["Living with uncertainty", "Responses to Hume remain debated, but his lesson is to hold beliefs with confidence proportional to the evidence.",
      "Many responses to Hume have been tried. Some use probability and Bayesian reasoning to show how evidence should raise or lower our confidence, though critics say these methods still need assumptions about the world. Others take a pragmatic line: if any method can predict the future, induction will, so it is our best bet. Some argue that demanding a non-circular justification is unreasonable, since the most basic forms of reasoning cannot all be proved without using themselves.\n\nImmanuel Kant said that reading Hume woke him from his 'dogmatic slumber'. Whatever view you take, Hume's lesson is practical: hold general beliefs with confidence proportional to the evidence, and stay open to the black swan."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the problem of induction?", ["We cannot observe anything at all about the past or present","Deduction always leads to false conclusions about the world","Science cannot make any predictions without perfect knowledge","Our confidence in the future rests on assuming nature will continue as before"], 3, "Experience alone cannot guarantee that the future will resemble the past."],
    [1, "Why is any argument for induction circular?", ["It relies on a deductive proof that turns out to be invalid","It depends on a contradiction that appears in picturing the future","It says induction worked before so will again, which itself relies on induction","It rests entirely on memories that are often unreliable"], 2, "Using induction to justify induction assumes the very thing in question."],
    [2, "What do the chicken and the black swan show?", ["Animals reason about the future better than people do","Observation is always misleading and should never be trusted","A pattern backed by many observations can be overturned by a single one","Rules drawn from experience are never useful in practice"], 2, "Expectations built on past experience can fail, so induction has limits."],
    [3, "How did Popper respond to Hume?", ["Induction can be proved correct if enough data is collected","Theories that survive testing are thereby proven true","Science advances by trying to falsify bold theories, not by confirming them","Science should avoid making any predictions about the future"], 2, "A single counterexample can refute a theory by deduction, with no need for induction."],
    [4, "What practical lesson does Hume leave us?", ["Hold beliefs with confidence matched to the evidence and stay open to surprises","Never trust any belief about the future, however well supported","Treat habit as a better guide than any available evidence","Stop using induction altogether in your daily life and work"], 0, "We cannot do without induction, but we should hold its conclusions with humility."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"You are sure the sun will rise tomorrow. Can you prove it? A swan says no."});

WB.L("veil-of-ignorance", "philosophy", "advanced", "The veil of ignorance: designing a fair society",
  "Imagine designing the rules of society without knowing whether you would be born rich or poor, healthy or sick. John Rawls's thought experiment reshaped debates about justice, and drew powerful critics who saw things very differently.",
  [
    ["Behind the veil", "John Rawls asked which rules people would choose for society if they did not know their own place in it.",
      "Imagine you are about to be born into a society, but you do not know who you will be. You might be born into wealth or poverty, with great talents or a serious disability, into a majority or a minority. Before you find out, you help choose the basic rules: how wealth is shared, which freedoms are protected, how opportunities are distributed.\n\nThis is the heart of the most famous thought experiment in modern political philosophy, set out by John Rawls in his 1971 book A Theory of Justice. Rawls called it the original position, with a 'veil of ignorance' hiding your sex, class, abilities, religion and idea of a good life. If you do not know whether you will be rich, you cannot rig the rules for the rich. The principles that emerge should be acceptable from every position."],
    ["Two principles", "Rawls argued for equal basic liberties and for allowing inequality only if it benefits the least advantaged.",
      "Rawls argued that people behind the veil would choose two principles. First, everyone gets an equal right to the most extensive basic liberties compatible with the same for others, such as free speech and conscience. These rank above the second principle. Second, inequalities are acceptable only if positions are open to all under fair equality of opportunity, and if they work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged. That last part is the difference principle.\n\nIt does not demand equal incomes. Paying some people more, say to encourage training, can be justified if it lifts up the worst-off. Rawls thought people would reason cautiously, focusing on the worst outcome: a 'maximin' strategy, maximising the minimum. The economist John Harsanyi argued that rational people would instead maximise average wellbeing."],
    ["Nozick's challenge", "Robert Nozick argued that justice depends on how holdings came about, not on matching a pattern of distribution.",
      "Rawls's most famous critic was his Harvard colleague Robert Nozick, whose 1974 book Anarchy, State, and Utopia defended a libertarian view. A distribution is just, he argued, if people acquired their holdings fairly and transferred them voluntarily, whatever pattern results.\n\nHe illustrated this with the basketball star Wilt Chamberlain. Suppose society begins with a distribution you consider perfectly fair, and then a million fans each happily pay a small sum to watch Chamberlain play. He becomes rich, and the pattern is upset. Yet every step was voluntary. How could the result be unjust? Nozick concluded that maintaining any fixed pattern would require continual interference in free choices, and that redistributive taxation risks treating people's labour as a resource others may claim."],
    ["Other critics", "Communitarians, feminists and capability theorists challenged Rawls from different directions.",
      "Criticism came from other angles too. Communitarians, such as Michael Sandel in a 1982 book, argued that the people behind the veil are 'unencumbered selves', stripped of the loyalties, traditions and communities that make us who we are.\n\nThe feminist philosopher Susan Moller Okin argued that Rawls had not taken justice within the family seriously enough, even though families shape children's opportunities so profoundly.\n\nThe economist and philosopher Amartya Sen, with the philosopher Martha Nussbaum, developed the capabilities approach. They argued that justice should focus less on resources and more on what people can actually do and be, since people with different needs convert the same income into very different lives."],
    ["A fairness test", "The veil of ignorance remains a practical fairness test, since you can test any rule by asking whether you would accept it without knowing which side of it you would end up on.",
      "Rawls refined his views over the following decades, notably in Political Liberalism in 1993, which asked how people with deeply different religious and moral beliefs can share a stable, fair society. His work and the responses to it frame much of today's argument about tax, welfare, health care and opportunity, across the political spectrum.\n\nEven people who reject his conclusions often find the veil of ignorance a useful thinking tool. When debating a policy, from school funding to disability support, ask: would I accept this rule if I did not know which side of it I would end up on? That question does not settle every disagreement, and reasonable people still reach different answers. But it pushes us to see a society from more than our own seat."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the purpose of the veil of ignorance?", ["To hide facts about economics from decision makers","To strip away bias so rules are fair from every position","To ensure everyone shares the same talents","To let the majority choose the rules"], 1, "If you do not know your place, you cannot rig the rules in your own favour."],
    [1, "What does the difference principle allow?", ["Equal incomes for every person, whatever their role or effort","Inequalities only if they work to the benefit of the least advantaged","Any inequality at all that helps to grow the economy","Inequalities that reward the most talented people most"], 1, "Inequality is justified only when it makes the worst-off better off than they would otherwise be."],
    [2, "What point does Nozick's Wilt Chamberlain example make?", ["Athletes should be paid less than other kinds of workers","Wealth should always be divided equally across a society","Fans should not be allowed to pay for entertainment","Voluntary exchanges break any set pattern of distribution"], 3, "Nozick held that justice depends on how holdings came about, not on matching a pattern."],
    [3, "What was Sandel's communitarian objection to the veil?", ["It gives too much weight to the role of the family","It ignores the need for economic growth in a society","It hands people far too many liberties to choose from","It imagines 'unencumbered selves' stripped of their loyalties"], 3, "Communitarians argue we cannot understand people apart from their communities."],
    [4, "How can the veil of ignorance be used in everyday debate?", ["Choose the rule that is favoured by the largest number of people","Choose the rule that would benefit you and your family most","Ask if you would accept a rule not knowing which side of it you would be on","Set aside the views of those who are directly affected by it"], 2, "It is a thinking tool for testing fairness, not a way to settle every disagreement."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Design a society without knowing who you will be in it. What would you choose?"});

